no tool roster can contradict. The first version of the check counted it and would have failed on a correct row.
The mutation that un-strikes row 4 goes red.

── ROUND 118 (probe): three refusals that stated the problem and not the fix ──── ROUND 118 (probe): three refusals that stated the problem and not the fix ──
Two properties were measured and both turned out NOT to be machine-checkable, which is worth recording as much as
a fix. "Every refusal names a next step" reported 102 of 116 refusals as silent, and spot-checking showed them
actionable in substance ("needs a title", "needs either sourcePath or sourceUrl") — a keyword list is not that
property. "Every tool description mentions each of its parameters" reported 50 of 75 unmentioned, and the
descriptions cover them in prose ("the project") — prose is not that property either. Both would have been checks
that report false positives, which round 142 already established is worse than the measurement it preserves.

So the round did something concrete instead: the three refusals that genuinely named no fix. The preview-profile
one — which exists in TWO places, `renderPreview` and `renderViews` — now names the field
(`renderProfiles.preview`), the operation that writes it (`{op: "render.profile.set"}`) and the fact that it
belongs to the revision rather than to the call. The contact-sheet one now says to run `blender_visual_review`
first and that the reviewer is handed bytes rather than a path.

Two existing assertions were tightened from "the message contains this phrase" to "the message names the fix",
and the mutation that deletes the fix goes red. Its first version changed only the FIRST of the two identical
sites while the case drives the second (`renderViews`), so it survived — the duplication itself was a reading.

── ROUND 130: no probe — a goal check-up, and the CI workflow audited step by step ──
No product code changed. Two measurements.

The CI workflow's steps were checked against what has actually been verified rather than against what looks
plausible: the Python prerequisite, the pinned DSH deployment, the workspace links (write and check), the preset
source, the contract layer, and — as its last step — `verify-clean-clone.mjs --source .`. Every one of them has
been run and passes in this checkout, which is as far as it can be taken here: the workflow targets
`ubuntu-latest`, and this machine has docker, podman and colima installed but none running, so starting one would
mean downloading a VM for a claim that is already backed by the same commands succeeding locally. The one half
that stays unverified is the operating system.

Then the goal's own evidence, re-measured: 64 contract files, 1489 self-counted assertions and 318 node:test
cases; run-all.sh 16/16; `verify:clone` exit 0 from a clean clone against a clean DSH_HOME with 64/64 inside the
clone; the four deployment checks all exit 0; 12355 product code lines with 35 dark (0.3%), each named; the
decision register complete from D1 to D197 with all 664 citations resolving.

The deviation register is twelve rows: eleven still open and one resolved — the procedural texture, whose compiler
half the parallel contributor wrote and which now has a real-Blender case. The eleven open ones are all deliberate
trade-offs with their reasons written down (the parts of the SPEC this project does not intend to implement), and
none of them is a gap of the kind the last thirty rounds cleared: referential integrity, cross-language field
names, concurrent shared resources, the subject of an approval, declared-versus-implemented, and everything the
documents name (sections, test files, decision numbers, source paths).

What remains is four decisions this session cannot make: making the repository public, adding the `dsh-plugin`
topic, confirming the monorepo entry form, and choosing a packaging direction for the four unpublished siblings.

── ROUND 129: no probe — the decision numbers the documents cite ──── ROUND 129: no probe — the decision numbers the documents cite ──
No product code changed. The documents cite decisions by number everywhere — 664 citations across nine documents —
and those numbers are the repository's memory, so a citation to a number nobody wrote down sends a reader nowhere
with no complaint from anything.

Finding out what "written down" means here took six wrong readings of this repository's own prose, and every one
of them was the same mistake in a different coat: ask which document and which spelling the mark refers to before
asking whether it exists. The decisions are defined in three shapes across two documents — `### D82 — title`
headings up to about D141, change-log rows `| … | D142：…` with a colon, and `runtime-audit.md`'s table rows
`| D1 | …` WITHOUT a colon — and the earliest ten are not in the architecture file at all: its own header says
"M0 的 D1–D10 见 runtime-audit.md §7", which is exactly the referent a shape-only check misses.

The intermediate readings are worth keeping: treating every `DNNN` as a definition reported zero dangling
(circular); row prefixes alone, 151; headings alone, 82; both, 8; both plus the second document, still 8 — because
that document's rows carry no colon. All three shapes and both documents: 197 decisions defined (D1 through D197
with no gap) and all 664 citations resolving.

The check lives in `docs-consistency.test.mjs`, with guards so a failed parse cannot silently become "everything
passes" (more than 100 definitions, at least 200 citations). Two mutations go red: a manual citing D999, and the
`D1` row in `runtime-audit.md` being renumbered.

It also taught itself one exclusion: the round that added it wrote, IN `milestone-status.md`, the sentence "cite a
D999 in a manual" — describing the mutation it had just run — and the check read its own documentation as a
dangling citation. The narrative is excluded now, for the same reason the test-file check excludes it: a record may
legitimately name a number that was never defined, while a manual may not, because a reader follows it.

── ROUND 128: no probe — the test files the documentation names ──── ROUND 128: no probe — the test files the documentation names ──
No product code changed. CONTRIBUTING.md keeps a table of "change this, and this assertion will catch the rest",
and each of its nine rows names the suite that does the catching. Those names are how a contributor finds the
check without running the whole suite, and a renamed or deleted suite leaves the table pointing at nothing — with
no complaint from anything, because a document is not executed.

Sweeping the live documents (README, CONTRIBUTING, the three manuals, security, tool-contracts) for every
`*.test.mjs` and `*.e2e.mjs` they name: 80 names, one that does not resolve. That one turned out to be my
extraction rather than the document — `tool-contracts.md` writes `tool-plane-m1/m2.e2e.mjs`, a shorthand for two
suites, which a filename regex reads literally. The document was not wrong, but it was unclear to a reader and to
a check alike, so it spells both names out now.

The narrative documents are excluded on purpose: `milestone-status.md` and `architecture-decisions.md` are
records, and a record naming a suite that has since been renamed is a true statement about the past — the same
distinction the counts sweep had to make in round 130.

The check asserts that every test file a live document names exists in one of the four test directories, with a
guard so that a failed parse cannot silently become "everything passes" (the documents must name at least twenty
suites). Two mutations go red: a renamed suite in the contributing table, and a renamed suite in a manual.

── ROUND 127: no probe — an audit of my own compliance with the repository's own rule ──── ROUND 127: no probe — an audit of my own compliance with the repository's own rule ──
No product code changed. `CONTRIBUTING.md` §5 requires that any measurement- or defect-driven decision be written
into `architecture-decisions.md`, numbered after the last D, with three things: what the decision is, the fact that
triggered it, and what would happen otherwise ("the reason you delete is the hardest information to recover").

Measuring my own recent commits against it: the newest row was D185 (round 124), and the twelve `fix(deepblend)`
commits after it — every one of them driven by a real defect — had no decision record at all. They do now, as
D186 through D197: the two previews of one revision deleting each other's image, the installable package's missing
client declaration and files list, two manifest entries for one asset path, a guard that silently skips when its
field is missing, a redirect walking around an approval, a provenance record naming the URL that did not answer,
an approval whose subject drifted in time, a declared asset hash nothing compared with the file, two encoders on
one MP4, a manifest de-duplicating by id while the artifact index de-duplicates by path, the peer-dependency rule
the ecosystem rejects submissions for, and an uninstall path whose first step only changed configuration.

Renumbering was needed too: the new rows started at D187 while D186 did not exist, so they moved down one and the
sequence now runs D185 → D186…D197 without a gap.

The lesson worth keeping: I had been FOLLOWING §5's format — the commit messages, the measurement in every round —
without ever checking back that each defect-driven change actually got its row. A repository's own rules are among
the few things that can be used to measure the person following them.

── ROUND 126: no probe — the manuals' cross-references, and two wrong extractions before a right one ──── ROUND 126: no probe — the manuals' cross-references, and two wrong extractions before a right one ──
No product code changed. The manuals cross-reference by name and section number ("SPEC §15.2",
"`milestone-status.md` §14") rather than by link — six relative links exist in the whole documentation set, and all
six resolve — which reads well and cannot break a build, and which means a stale number sends a reader nowhere
with no complaint from anything. Two things make it checkable: the referent is named in the citation, and both
documents are in this repository.

Getting there took two wrong extractions, both exposed by their own output. The first compared every `§N` against
the milestone log's sections and reported twelve missing — most of which were SPEC sections (the same "read which
document the citation names" mistake as round 113). The second read the SPEC properly but reported seventy-one
missing, because the SPEC numbers its TOP-LEVEL sections in Chinese: `## 十五、安全与权限` IS §15. With the mapping
in place, all 92 SPEC citations and all 20 milestone citations resolve.

The check reads both tables (Arabic sub-sections plus the Chinese top-level headings; the milestone log's `##`
sections), then checks only the citations that NAME their referent, with guards so that a failed parse cannot
silently become "everything passes": both documents must yield more than fifty sections and the check must compare
more than fifty citations. Two mutations go red: a SPEC citation pointing at §99.9, and a milestone citation
pointing at §999.

── ROUND 117 (probe): the uninstall path the manual gives did not uninstall ──── ROUND 117 (probe): the uninstall path the manual gives did not uninstall ──
The probe's number is unchanged (35 dark lines, 0.3%) because the change is in a TOOL rather than in the product's
libraries — the coverage probe measures the product packages.

`install.md` §6 is the operator's removal path, and running its first step in a throwaway home showed it does not
do what the section implies: `plugin:install --portable` empties the operator layer (its own comment says so) and
leaves the bundle composed, so the plugin is still installed and `dsh` still mounts its three rows.

Following that found a second defect: the ecosystem's own removal command could not finish the job either.
MEASURED: `dsh plugin remove @deepblend/dsh-blender-bundle --profile web` fails with
ERR_PNPM_CANNOT_REMOVE_MISSING_DEPS, because `install-plugin.mjs` wrote `dsh.profile.bundles` — all the Loader
needs — and no `dependencies` entry, which is what pnpm (and therefore `plugin remove`) reads. The ecosystem's
`plugin add` writes both keys, so the two install paths produced different profile states.

The installer writes both now, as its own drift check: `plugin:check` reports the missing entry, `--check` exits 1,
and an install adds it. Measured end to end: the real home reported the drift, one install fixed it, and
`dsh plugin remove` now exits 0 with `bundles: []` — the plugin is actually removed. The manual's section names
the removal command first and says why it used to fail.

A new case runs the round trip (install, remove with the ecosystem's command, the profile no longer composes the
bundle), and the mutation that stops the installer writing the dependency entry goes red on it.

One slip of my own: the first version of the dependency check sat AFTER the `if (checkOnly) { … process.exit(0) }`
block, so it never ran in check mode — and the real home reported exit 0 while its `dependencies` was empty. What
exposed it was the contradiction between that reading and the file's own contents.

── ROUND 125: no probe — mounting the plugin must start nothing ──── ROUND 125: no probe — mounting the plugin must start nothing ──
No product code changed. Round 124 turned "importing the module does nothing" into a check; the other half of the
reviewer's "surprising install-time behaviour" is what happens when a profile MOUNTS the plugin — and this
plugin's job is launching an external binary, so "does mounting it start a Blender" is a real question. The
plugin's own comment claims the runtime row "parks in `waiting`" rather than probing at mount, and nothing
asserted it: the activation suite checks the three rows, the two services and the tool plane, but not that the
mount started no process.

The suite now counts `pgrep -f Blender` before the rows are mounted and again after, and requires the two counts
to be equal. Counting processes rather than asking this process about its handles is deliberate: the question is
what the MOUNT did, not what happens to be running on the machine, and the difference of two counts removes the
latter. The mutation that makes the host launch a detached Blender-named process while being constructed goes red
on exactly this check (`{"before":0,"after":1}`).

The first mutation attempt used `spawnSync('sleep')`, which neither matches `pgrep -f Blender` nor survives the
call (spawnSync waits), so it broke an unrelated assertion first — the check needed a detached process whose argv
carries the name.

The README's numbers do not move: this assertion lives in the COMPOSITION layer, while `count-assertions.mjs`
counts the contract layer's thirty-two self-counting files. I changed the README to 1490 first and only the
counter's own output showed it was still 1489 — which is the lesson: which bucket a new assertion lands in is a
measurement, not an assumption.

── ROUND 124: no probe — the reviewer's last machine-checkable question ──── ROUND 124: no probe — the reviewer's last machine-checkable question ──
No product code changed. The reviewer checklist has eight items; seven are human judgement or already covered by
this repository's checks. The one machine-checkable item nobody had checked is §A7.5's last clause — "surprising
install-time behaviour" — which is a property: a plugin that writes files, starts processes or opens sockets the
moment it is imported is doing something the person installing it did not ask for. These six packages are supposed
to be inert on import; they publish services, register tools and export functions, and all of that happens when the
profile composes them.

The case runs each import in a child process whose cwd and HOME are throwaway directories, compares the cwd's
contents before and after, and checks that no handle was left open. The stronger version is the right one: the
first attempt imported in-process and looked at `getActiveResourcesInfo`, which reported zero for all six packages
— and would also have reported zero for a module that wrote a file, which makes it a check that cannot fail. The
mutation that makes the host package write a file at import time goes red against the directory comparison.

One slip on the way: the child's cwd is the throwaway directory, so bare package specifiers could not resolve and
all six imports reported failure. Importing by absolute path fixes it — the module's own imports still walk up to
the repository's `node_modules`.

That closes the reviewer checklist: nothing in it is left unchecked that a machine can check.

── ROUND 123: no probe — the command a user actually types ──── ROUND 123: no probe — the command a user actually types ──
No product code changed. Every previous check looked at the plugin's pieces — the manifest's fields, the patch's
rows, the configuration each row carries — and none of them ran `dsh plugin add`, which is the command the plugin
list tells a user to run and the one place where a missing `dsh.client`, a `files` list omitting the patch, or a
row whose configuration never reaches the profile would appear as a plugin that installs and does nothing.

`contract/plugin-install-path.test.mjs` builds a throwaway `$DSH_HOME`, installs the bundle into it with the real
command, and dumps the composed profile. Three assertions: the bundle is in the profile's bundle list, all three
rows compose, and EVERY row arrives carrying its configuration (`timeoutMs: 180000`, `maxSpillBytes: 67108864`,
`maxPreviewSamples: 512`). The third is the end-to-end proof of round 121's claim that this bundle composes
configuration rather than being a meta-package — the question a reviewer asks, answered by the real installer
instead of by reading the patch. A final case proves the real `~/.dsh` was never written, by checking that it
still composes the bundle.

The local directory is used rather than a `github:` spec because that form cannot be exercised from here: the
repository is still private and pnpm resolves `github:` through an anonymous codeload tarball, which answers 404
— the failure the research document measured live. The local path takes the same code path (pnpm install, then the
bundle's patch composed into the profile) without the network, so everything except the fetch is covered.

Two mutations go red: the runtime row losing its configuration, and the UI row disappearing from the patch. The
new file moved the derived counts again and the counts test caught it: 63 → 64 contract files, 311 → 313 node:test
