── ROUND 117: no probe — a correction: the texture WAS wired, my case asked for no effect ──── ROUND 117: no probe — a correction: the texture WAS wired, my case asked for no effect ──
No product code changed. Round 115 measured the node graph the compiler builds for a textured material and found
the pattern's output connected to nothing, which was written up as "it reads it, builds it, and does not connect
it" and folded into deviation §7 #14. That conclusion was WRONG.

Reading the whole of `_build_texture_graph` — the first pass had read half of it — shows the pattern feeds three
OPT-IN consumers: `bump` drives the Principled's Normal through a Bump node, `roughnessVariation` swings its
Roughness through a Map Range, and `colorVariation` tints Base Color. The case declared `{type: 'noise',
scale: 12.5}` — none of the three — so the compiler built the chain and connected it to nothing, correctly. The
code was right and the input was empty: the same slip as round 118 (inferring behaviour from a field name) and
round 142 (treating source shape as the property), and this time it was reading half a function.

Declaring `bump: 0.6, roughnessVariation: 0.3` produces, measured in real Blender:

    TEX_NOISE.Factor -> BUMP.Height
    BUMP.Normal -> BSDF_PRINCIPLED.Normal
    TEX_NOISE.Factor -> MAP_RANGE.Value
    MAP_RANGE.Result -> BSDF_PRINCIPLED.Roughness

M1 Blender integration: 80/80. The assertion names those four links exactly rather than matching "something to
something", which would pass on a graph wired to the wrong socket — the failure the case exists to catch. The
deviation row now says what is true: implemented in the working tree, proven by a test, and the only thing still
open is that the contributor has not committed it. Their file was not committed here; that is theirs to do.

── ROUND 116: two manifest entries for one path ──── ROUND 116: two manifest entries for one path ──
The asset destination is `assets/raw/<file name>` — shared by NAME, not by asset id — so two ingests of one file
name write the same path. Measured with two sources that share a name and differ in bytes (identical bytes would
make a torn write invisible): both succeeded, the file that landed was one of the two intact, and the manifest's
hash matched NEITHER. The hash was not miscomputed: the manifest kept TWO entries for that path, the first
describing bytes the second had replaced, because its de-duplication filter looked at `assetId` alone. The
artifact index already replaces by PATH ("re-emitting the same path replaces its entry"), so this was one fact
kept in two places that disagreed.

The filter now takes the path into account, and the copy goes through a temporary name and a rename (the
discipline `writeJsonAtomic` applies everywhere else). The fix immediately turned an older case red — the one
written in round 108 asserting that BOTH entries land — which means that case had been pinning the defect; it now
asserts the honest property (both succeed, one entry for the shared path, and that entry's file exists).

Two mutations: filtering by id alone goes red, and putting the in-place copy back SURVIVES — measured as an
equivalent mutant, because the whole stretch from the copy to the manifest write is synchronous and two calls
cannot interleave in it. The rename is therefore DEFENSIVE, and the code says so: it makes the file atomic for a
future reader (a panel, a second process, a tool opening the asset mid-ingest) where the synchronous argument no
longer holds. The three new dark lines are named at their sites for the same reason — a catch reachable only on a
full volume, and a post-copy size check that is a second belt behind two earlier ones.

── ROUND 115: two encoders, one file ──── ROUND 115: two encoders, one file ──
`encodedPath(jobDirectory, jobId)` is one path per job, and an export is not instantaneous — it shells out to
ffmpeg and then probes the result. So a second export of the same job writes the same MP4 while the first is still
writing it, and each verifies a file the other is producing. Nothing guarded it: both callers see a `completed`
job and both proceed. MEASURED with two concurrent `_deliverJob({reason: 'export'})` calls: BOTH failed with
`ENCODE_VERIFY_FAILED` — a message that blames the video's properties rather than the collision.

The host now keeps an in-flight map keyed `projectId/jobId`: the second caller is refused with the new
`EXPORT_IN_PROGRESS`, naming which call is encoding, into which file, and what to do instead. The guard wraps the
whole body (`_deliverJob` delegating to `_deliverJobBody`) and clears the flag in a `finally` — a leaked flag would
turn one race into a permanently undeliverable job, which is the worse failure. Two mutations go red: removing the
guard, and never clearing the flag.

The case itself slipped twice, both caught by readings: the two frames were written as the SAME PNG, and ffmpeg's
image2 encoder collapses identical frames, so the encode produced one frame and failed verification for a reason
unrelated to the case (a cousin of round 71's pure-colour-frame lesson). The assertions were narrowed to the guard
itself — exactly one caller refused with the collision named, and a LATER export not refused by a flag the first
one left behind — which is the property under test and does not depend on ffmpeg's output.

