── ROUND 111: a declared hash nothing compared with the file ──
The asset workflow is two steps: `blender_asset_ingest` COMPUTES the sha256 of what it wrote and reports it, and
the caller then DECLARES it in `asset.add`. Nothing compared the two. The patch applier is a pure function over
documents — no filesystem, which is the right shape for it — so it stored whatever hash the caller supplied, and
the Python never reads `assets[].sha256` at all (measured: zero occurrences). A patch could therefore write
provenance that is simply false into the scene, and the delivery manifest and any human auditing where the bytes
came from would believe it.

The check lives in the transaction, the one layer holding both the document and the store: after applying a patch,
every `asset.add` whose file EXISTS is hashed and compared, and a mismatch is refused with `ASSET_HASH_MISMATCH`
naming both numbers, committing nothing. A path with no file is deliberately left to the compiler — "declare it
before the bytes exist" is a different question, and refusing it here would break a flow that works, which the
third mutation checks by breaking it.

Three mutations go red: removing the comparison, comparing something to itself, and refusing a missing file.

── ROUND 110: no probe — a check that was written three times and removed ──
No product code changed, and no assertion stayed either. The round applied the previous three rounds' ruler in its
POSITIVE form: seven host methods default a revision (`request.revision ?? record.currentRevision`), which is
right, and the property that makes it safe is that each one RETURNS the revision it used so the answer is
self-describing. MEASURED: all seven do.

Pinning that by reading the host's source failed three times for one reason — source shape is not the property.
Version one looked for `revision` anywhere in the method body and survived the mutation that removed it from the
answer (each method also mentions it in a message or a call). Version two narrowed to `return { … }` objects
carrying `projectId` and saw four of twenty-one literals, the rest being nested, while `validateScene` hands its
fields to `toCanonicalQAReport({ … })`. Version three accepted `({` as a shape too, which caught that method and
broke three others whose `projectId` and `revision` legitimately live in different literals.

The check is gone and the measurement is recorded in its place, together with where the behavioural half already
lives: the orchestration suite requires a preview to report which revision it wrote into, and the tool-plane suite
requires the approval prompt to name the revision and the granted retry to render that one. A source-level check
that keeps reporting false positives is worse than the measurement it was meant to preserve.

── ROUND 109: the approval's subject drifted in TIME rather than across a redirect ──── ROUND 109: the approval's subject drifted in TIME rather than across a redirect ──
The same shape as round 107 and 108, one step further out. The delivery render's approval gate refuses above a
threshold, and the tool plane answers it by asking the operator and then re-issuing the same request with
`approved: true`. Two details made that approval's subject movable: the prompt's sentence named the frame count and
range but NOT the revision, and `revision` is optional in the schema ("defaults to the project's current
revision") — so a patch landing while a human read the prompt meant the retry could render a revision nobody
approved.

The prompt names the revision now (only when the host reported one, matching the same sentence's existing rule
about the range), and the retry is PINNED to the revision the refusal measured: `cause.detail?.revision ??
startRequest.revision`. Measured along the way: `requestApproval` sends only `{ agent, toolName, callId, reason,
signal }` to the operator — no structured detail — so the sentence is the channel, which is where a person reads it
anyway. Three mutations go red: not pinning, not naming, and pinning the request's own revision instead of the
approved one.

── ROUND 108: the provenance record still named the URL that did not answer ──
One rung further along round 107's ladder. That round made the redirect chain visible in the TOOL RESULT
(`redirectedFrom`); this one asked what the record a later reader trusts says — and `assets/manifest.json` stored
only the approved URL (`source: { kind: 'url', url: sourceUrl }`). The tool result is gone when the conversation
ends, so after a redirect the manifest could not answer the very question a provenance record exists for.

The entry carries both now: `url` is what was asked for, `resolvedUrl` is what answered, and the second appears
only when the chain actually moved. Two mutations go red: dropping `resolvedUrl`, and setting it to the approved
URL so it looks present while saying nothing. `assets/manifest.json` has no published schema — it is a store
artifact, unlike scene-spec, scene-patch and job-result — so nothing needed syncing there.

── ROUND 107: an approval a redirect could walk around ──
The remote-asset fetch validated only the ORIGINAL url's protocol and then used `redirect: 'follow'`, which hands
the decision to the network. So the URL a human approves is not necessarily the URL the request reaches: a server
answering `302 Location: http://127.0.0.1:3080/…` moves it to this machine's own services, and neither the host's
rule nor the operator's eyes were on that hop. The approval gate itself is real — it refused the first version of
these very cases — but the redirect made its subject a lie.

Redirects are followed one hop at a time now, each hop under the same protocol rule, bounded at five hops, and the
chain is part of the result (`redirectedFrom: { hops, chain }`), so where the bytes came from is visible again.
Loopback is deliberately NOT refused, and the comment says why: the approval gate is the control for "fetch this
URL", local asset servers are legitimate, and this suite's own remote cases serve from 127.0.0.1. What was missing
was not a ban but the ability to see.

Four mutations go red: handing redirects back to the network, removing the hop limit, removing the per-hop
protocol check, and not reporting the chain.

The first reading of this round was 41 dark lines — the new code's error arms. Two of them are drivable and got
cases (a 3xx with no `Location`; a `Location` that is not a URL — relative values are legal and resolve, so only a
genuinely broken header reaches that arm), and the third is named at the site (cancelling a body this process owns
does not fail; it is a catch because an unhandled rejection would take the process down). The second reading is
back to 32.

