── ROUND 112: no probe — a projection whose every read is `?? null`-guarded ──
No product code changed. The revision manifest carries a projection of the compile report's technical validation
(`ok`, `errors`, `counts`, `geometry`, `frameRange`, `fps`, `engine`, `activeCamera`, `animatedObjects`,
`cameraParameters`), and every one of those reads ends in `?? null` — so a renamed field does not fail, it puts
`null` in the manifest and the panel shows a revision whose technical validation is partly blank.

Two halves, and the second is the stronger one. A source-level tie holds the validator's returned keys (13 of
them, extracted from `validate_scene`'s own body) against the fields the host projects (10, extracted from
`technical.X` reads) — nothing projected that the validator does not emit. And a behavioural case drives the
passthrough with values no default can produce (`fps: 24`, `engine: 'CYCLES'`, `frameRange: [1, 48]`), because
"it passes the field through" cannot be shown by an input whose value equals the fallback (round 107's lesson).

Three times the instrument misled me first: the extraction caught a NESTED helper's `return {` (seven keys, none
of them the report's) until it was bounded by the function body; the case used `saveCheckpoint: false`, which
skips the compile altogether, so the manifest's validation is legitimately null there; and — the one worth
keeping — the first version of the tie compared only the READ fields, so the mutation that renames the projected
OUTPUT key (`cameraParametersX: technical.cameraParameters`) survived it. Adding the pair assertion (every key
must be named after the field it reads) is what made it fail. A renamed output key with an unchanged read is
exactly what a one-sided check cannot see.

── ROUND 111: no probe — the result envelope's VERSION was checked, its field names were not ──
No product code changed. Every action's answer travels as one document written by `bootstrap.py` and read by the
provider and the host (`envelope.warnings`, `notices`, `result`, `status`, `capabilities`). What the runtime
checks is `protocolVersion`, which catches a whole-document generation change but not a renamed FIELD: an
envelope that keeps its version and renames `notices` leaves the host reading `undefined`, so a render is
reported with no warnings at all — silence that looks like success.

The names are held against each other now, the same way the compile report's fingerprint is (round 106):
`pythonEmits: [action, capabilities, error, jobId, notices, protocolVersion, result, status, warnings]` against
`hostReads: [protocolVersion, status, error, capabilities, result, warnings, notices]`, with no field read that
the envelope does not carry, plus a check that the error envelope holds the `code` and `message` the provider
turns into a coded failure.

Two process notes worth keeping: the first mutation renamed the dict at the TOP of `build_envelope`, where
`notices` is not set (it is assigned further down), so the mutation survived until every assignment was renamed;
and the block was first placed above the `python` discovery — the same placement mistake as the previous round,
caught this time by reading the error rather than by moving it twice.

