WHAT THE CASE FOUND, and it was a real one: `renderPreview` staged into `<revision>/.render-staging/<camera>.png`
— one directory per REVISION — and the cleanup removed the whole directory. Two previews of one revision at once
therefore made whichever finished first delete the other's image, and the other reported `RENDER_NO_OUTPUT` for
a render that had worked (reading: `{"left":"…frame61-camera-main.png","right":"RENDER_NO_OUTPUT: The Blender
renderer reported success but wrote no image."}`), with the artifact index missing the entry. The concurrency
suite's preview case used two DIFFERENT revisions, which is why it never saw this.

The staging is now per RENDER (`<revision>/.render-staging/<jobId>/` — the job id is minted and written as a
`running` record inside one synchronous block, so it is unique per call), the cleanup removes only its own
layer, and `rmdirSync` on the parent is the emptiness check (it refuses a non-empty directory, so no listing and
no race). One assertion from round 109 was moved to the new reality rather than relaxed: the half-written frame
is now written by the STUB at the path the host handed it, which is what a real renderer does before it dies.

── ROUND 103: still no probe — a property that was already true, now asserted ──
