── ROUND 100: the compiled scene of a delivery render was never deleted ──
Following the previous round's leak, every `removeTree(` in the host was checked. Two of them remove a COMPILED
SCENE (preview and views, each in a `finally`), and the third — `checkpoint.compiled` — belongs to
`_renderViewPlan`, NOT to the delivery path. Reading is not asserting, so an assertion was written for the
preview path first (with a guard proving the compile really happened), and then for the delivery path.

THE FIRST VERSION OF THAT ASSERTION WAS ATTACHED TO THE WRONG CASE: a revision that HAS a checkpoint, where no
compile happens at all, so `tmp/` was empty and the check passed over nothing. Moved to a case that really
compiles (no checkpoint, delivery fails on a frame-count mismatch, with `world.compiles()` as the guard), the
reading was immediate:

    {"compiles":1,"status":"failed","leftovers":["render-r0002-…"]}

A delivery render's compiled scene had never been deleted by anything.

BUT DELETING IT ON EVERY PATH WOULD BE WRONG, and that is the interesting half: `resumeRenderJob` reuses
`record.checkpointPath`, so removing it would turn "continue this render" into "the renderer cannot open its
scene". `completed` is the one status from which nothing resumes — before it the compiled `.blend` is LIVE
STATE, after it it is garbage — so the cleanup lives in `_driveRender`'s `finally` and runs only for a completed
job. Three assertions (a failed delivery keeps it; a resume reuses it without compiling again; a completed one
removes it) plus a guard that the helper removes only `<workspace>/tmp/render-*` and never a revision's own
checkpoint — and each of the two path guards needed its own input, since dropping only the `tmp` one left every
other case green until a "looks like a scratch but is not under tmp" directory was handed in.

Five mutations, four red; the fifth is an EQUIVALENT mutant (dropping only the name guard changes no behaviour
for any input the tests use), which is the "aim the mutation with context" lesson again.

── ROUND 99: a successful fetch leaked its scratch, found by asking the other half ──
The round asked a concurrency question (two remote ingests in flight at once) and, in writing it, broke two
existing assertions in the same file. One of them was named "the scratch space of every FAILED fetch is gone"
and was telling the truth — but nobody had ever asked about the SUCCESSFUL half:

    scratch dirs after one SUCCESSFUL remote ingest: [ 'asset-f0624b5e-…' ]

Every successful remote ingest left the bytes it had just downloaded under `<workspace>/tmp/asset-<uuid>/`,
a second copy of a file already in `assets/raw/`, growing without bound. The failure path had always cleaned
up; the happy path never did. The fix wraps the tail of `ingestAsset` in a `try/finally` that removes only
`fetchedScratch`, which is set for a REMOTE source and never for a local one — the caller's own file is not
ours to delete, and there is an assertion for that side too.

Product code changed, so this column is a fresh reading: 53 dark lines, unchanged in count — the fix added a
`finally` (and three lines of product code) while the dark set is the same 32 named lines plus the parallel
contributor's 21.

