── ROUND 97: a measurement that was wrong, and a parser that was in the wrong place ──
`tool-contracts.md`'s per-tool tables and the tool definitions are two descriptions of one surface, and only one
of them can be wrong without anybody noticing: a parameter added to a tool is invisible in the manual (the model
reads the schema), while a row for a parameter that no longer exists reads as a capability.

The first measurement was WRONG: it took only the FIRST table in each section and reported `scene_patch` as
missing `actor`/`stage` and `preview_render` as missing `width`/`height` — all four are in the SECOND table of the
same section. Reading every table, the five documented tables match the tool definitions exactly. That is the
fourth time this session that the ruler needed calibrating before the thing it measured, which is why the check
added here matters less for what it found today than for what it will find tomorrow.

The parser for `defineTool` blocks lived inside `tool-parameters.test.mjs`, and the new check needed the same
facts — and a copied parser is the worst kind of copy, because the two drift silently and a test that parses
nothing looks exactly like one that passes. It now lives in `tests/lib/tool-definitions.mjs` and both checks use
it. The new check requires every parameter table in §3 to equal its tool's declared set (only sections that HAVE
a table; at least five tables must be compared or the check is nearly vacuous).

A mutation that added a parameter to `blender_job_status` stayed GREEN, and that is by design rather than a hole:
that tool is described in prose, and §3 expands only the tools whose contract is subtle. The limitation is now
written next to the check, because "the check passed" and "the check looked" are different statements.

Three mutations, two red; the one that was not is the limitation, recorded.

── ROUND 96: a drift that would have stopped the plugin mounting ──
The round started by timing the four install checks (all fast: 294 / 483 / 244 / 142 ms) and found `plugin:check`
reporting DRIFT: `profiles/web/cordis.patch.yml` was stale, because round 96 had deleted
`serveCachedCapabilities` from the bundle while the GENERATED operator layer still carried it. That layer is
derived (`npm run plugin:install` writes it; a patch layer's `config` replaces rather than merges, D74), so the
new configuration guard added in the same round would have REFUSED to mount the plugin at the next restart — a
user-visible breakage produced by this session's own change, invisible to every suite because the drift lives in
operator state rather than in the repository.

The environment was fixed first (`npm run plugin:install`; `plugin:check` now reports installed), and then the
failure itself was made self-explaining: the refusal now says that the usual cause is not a typo but a STALE
GENERATED LAYER, and gives the command that regenerates it and the restart that follows. A failure that only
appears at restart time has to carry its own fix, because it fires a long way from the edit that caused it. Two
mutations (delete the sentence; misspell the command) both go red, and `install.md` gained the matching advice:
run `plugin:check` after pulling new code.

This is the third appearance of the operator-state theme in three rounds: do not promise what the store
contains, do not promise which revision it is at, and remember that what is GENERATED from the repository goes
stale in a place nobody is looking.

── ROUND 95: a sentence about OPERATOR STATE, which this repository cannot keep true ──
The README's demo section said "当前项目已推进到 r0023" and pointed a reader at r0019–r0023's operation
manifests. The store says otherwise: `watch-commercial` holds r0001 and r0002, and the promised revisions are
not on disk at all. `render-job.js`'s header had the same shape ("49 of them are already on disk in the demo
project") where the measured number was four.

The root cause is not a stale number, it is the KIND of sentence: `.deepblend/projects/` is OPERATOR STATE, not
repository content — what it holds depends on which commands have been run — so "what it holds right now" is a
claim this repository has no way to keep true. It is the same rule the README already follows for milestone
status (there is a check for that one), applied to the store.

The fix is to state what the TOOLS do, because that IS checkable: the README now names
`apply-brief-content.mjs` and the range it walks (r0018 → r0023), and a new check requires the demo section to
name the tool and its range while the TOOL'S OWN HEADER must contain both ids in that order — plus a guard that
the store-state sentence cannot come back at all (a quotation of it is refused too, which is why the explanation
in the README has to talk around it).

The first version of that check searched the whole tool source and a mutation walked straight through it:
changing the README's range to `r0018 → r0029` stayed green because `r0029` appears in a label comment
somewhere else in the file. Searching the header, in order, turned both that mutation and a reversed range red.
Searching broadly is the same as not searching.

Five mutations, all red. Contract layer 61 files, 1427 assertions, 289 node:test cases (+1); product code lines
dark 32 (0.3%).

── ROUND 94: re-measuring the unreachability claims, second pass ──
The round went over the standing claims in the files that had not been re-measured, and three of the four
came back with something worth changing.

`project-store.js`'s index-write `catch {}` was tried with a FOURTH seam: making the index file itself
read-only (`chmod 0444`). The write SUCCEEDED — the mtime moved and the second project landed in the index —
because `writeFileAtomic` writes a temporary file and renames it, and POSIX lets a rename replace a read-only
file when its directory is writable. The "cannot be driven" conclusion holds, but the reason had to be written
correctly, and all three measured-and-rejected seams are now in the naming comment.

`json-schema.js`'s `default: return false` is unreachable because compilation refuses an unknown type name
(already asserted); the other half is now asserted too — all seven JSON type names are validated positively
(each accepts its own value and refuses another type's), because "the switch has seven cases" is a fact about
today's source while "these seven values are judged correctly" is the promise. The mutation that removes a case,
and the one that makes a case accept everything, both go red.

TWO `closeSync` CATCHES in `render-journal.js` and `frame-ledger.js` were commented `/* already closed */`,
which describes a situation that cannot occur: the descriptor is opened by that function and nothing else closes
it. The real reason is that a throw inside `finally` REPLACES the error being propagated, and the caller needs
the read failure rather than a close failure. A sentence describing an impossible situation sends the next
reader looking for a path that is not there.

And `png.js`'s unreachable `readPixel` default threw the SAME sentence as the header check — two copies of one
refusal in a file whose rule is one copy. It now says what it can only mean (an internal bug: the decoder
refused this type before reading any pixel), and the naming comment in `png-sheet.test.mjs` that QUOTED the old
sentence was corrected too: a quotation rots with the thing it quotes. The mutation that puts the old sentence
back survives every behavioural assertion, because the branch is unreachable — so a source-level assertion was
added (the refusal appears once as a thrown sentence; quoting it in a comment is fine, and is how the guard
explains why it is not repeating it). An unreachable branch can only be pinned at the source.

Five mutations, four red, and the one that was not is what produced the source-level assertion.

── ROUND 93: a dark line explained away as unreachable, which was reachable ──
The round went back over the standing "this line cannot be reached" claims — two earlier rounds had a guard find
ME first, so this time the guards were asked directly. Starting with the six dark lines in `scene-patch.js`,
which round 57 had named "the `PATCH_ID_INVALID` branch, shadowed by the schema's `pattern`", and which D154
closed with "this file is done: every line either executes or is proven unreachable".

Ten shapes were fed through `validateScenePatch` one at a time. Nine are shadowed exactly as described. The
tenth is not:

    REACHES  entity.material.set materialId  -> ["PATCH_ID_INVALID"]

`materialId` has no `pattern` in the patch schema, so for that shape the SEMANTIC branch is what answers — those
six lines were not dead code, they were reachable, and no test had ever touched them. A dark line explained away
as unreachable is the one kind of dark line that can hide a real defect, and this one had been explained away in
two places for forty rounds.

The test was written (`PATCH_ID_INVALID`, with the path naming `operations[0].materialId` and the message quoting
the grammar), which turns all six lines bright: `scene-patch.js` is now genuinely finished at 0/638 dark. The old
