── ROUND 92: the practice this repository runs on was missing from its contributor guide ──
`milestone-status.md` mentions mutation testing 174 times; `CONTRIBUTING.md` mentioned it ZERO times, and the
coverage probe not at all. A stranger reading the contributor guide would never learn how this project decides
what to test next — which is the practice the last six rounds have been running on, and which found a real
defect in every one of them.

Two sections were added: how to read the coverage probe (the three commands, what "product CODE lines" means,
that a dark line is a QUESTION rather than a defect, that the tree must not move during a probe, and that the
instrument has a known blind spot — a multi-line ternary buys a false dark line, so confirm with a
`process.stderr.write` before believing one), and the mutation discipline (a surviving mutation is a question,
aim mutations with context, one-sided checks of two-sided questions are invisible, and to prove "I did nothing"
the input must carry a value the default cannot equal).

CONTRIBUTING.md was also added to the list of manuals whose commands are checked — a contributor guide that
names a command which cannot be run is the same defect as an install manual doing it.

AND THEN A MUTATION DID NOT GO RED, pointing at a hole in the checker itself: renaming a documented command to
`npm run test:gone                     # 必须绿` was not caught, because the extractor's pattern for
`npm run <script>` ended with `\s*$` — a whole line of nothing else. This repository's manuals habitually put a
comment after a command, so ANY command with a trailing comment was unchecked, and a guide could name a script
that does not exist and stay green. The pattern now allows a trailing comment (the safer direction: naming a
command is a claim that it exists, and a false positive is a line the author can reword), and the extractor got
its own test — the checker is checked — which the mutation that restores `\s*$` turns red.

Four mutations, all red. Contract layer 61 files, 1424 assertions, 288 node:test cases (+1); run-all.sh 16/16
suites green.

── ROUND 91: a published contract that described something else ──
The round started by measuring which files nothing references — one hit, and it was the ruler's fault (Python's
`from deepblend_capabilities import …` omits the extension). Following that habit to `job-result.schema.json`:
mirrored, documented, referenced by SPEC, and **validated against nothing by any code or test**.

So the real records were put through it. All three demo projects, 45 attempt logs:

    records: 45   failing the shipped schema: 45

Every one. The `artifacts[]` definition permitted none of `viewId`, `role`, `at`, `iteration`, `columns`, `rows`,
`slot` or `views`; `kind` had neither `view` nor `contact-sheet`; `action` had no `render_views`; and the
top-level `warnings` array the records have always carried did not exist in the schema at all. A published
contract describing something other than what the product writes is worse than no contract, because a reader
trusts it — and nothing was running it, which is why nobody noticed.

The schema was corrected FROM MEASUREMENT rather than from guessing (`slot` was first written as an integer and
is in fact the view id string), and 45/45 pass now. Three checks were added to `host-render-orchestration.test.mjs`:
a successful record, a failed one, and one carrying VIEW and CONTACT-SHEET artifacts must each validate against
the schema the product publishes — read from DISK rather than through `getJob`, whose projection is a different
document (the first version fed the projection in and failed on a missing `schemaVersion`, which is exactly the
difference).

One mutation survived at first and pointed at the sample: renaming `viewId` in the schema left every check green,
because none of the records being validated carried a view artifact. Validating a schema needs the sample with
the most fields in it; with that record added, all three view-artifact mutations go red.

── ROUND 90: a dangling $ref, and advice that could not be followed ──
The same ruler as the two rounds before, moved down a plane: does every field the SceneSpec schema declares
get read by the compiler or the contracts? Three came back — `license.commercialUse`, `license.attribution`,
`asset.license` — and following `asset.license` found two defects that were worse than "nobody reads it":

  1. `scene-patch.schema.json` declared `asset.add.asset.license` as `{"$ref": "#/$defs/license"}` while that
     document had NO such definition. So a patch declaring an asset's licence did not get REFUSED — the
     validator threw `SchemaDefinitionError` from inside itself. An exception a caller cannot branch on, where
     the contract promises a code.
  2. The advice the round before had just added to `blender_asset_ingest`'s answer printed
     `license: "CC-BY-4.0"` (a bare string), while `asset.license` is an OBJECT (`source`, `commercialUse`,
     `attribution`). A model that did what it was told got `SCENE_PATCH_INVALID` — for doing what it was told.

Both are fixed: the `license` definition was copied into the patch schema (the validator resolves references
within one document), the advice now spells `license: {"source":"CC-BY-4.0"}`, and `contract/schema-refs.test.mjs`
asserts every `$ref` in every shipped schema RESOLVES — the general form of the defect — plus that a licensed
declaration is accepted, that the bare-string spelling is rejected, and that the advice sentence still uses the
schema's shape. The loop that matters most is in `host-asset-ingest.test.mjs`: the declaration the answer TELLS
the caller to write is fed back through `validateScenePatch` and must pass. Advice that cannot be followed is
worse than no advice.

ADDING THAT CHECK RAN INTO A GUARD THIS REPOSITORY ALREADY HAD, which is the second time this round the guard
found me first: `contract/schema-mirror.test.mjs` asserts the packaged schemas are byte-identical to the
authoritative ones under `deepblend/schemas/`, and I had edited only the mirror — it reported a 357-byte
difference immediately, which is exactly the defect its header describes (a drifted mirror means the runtime
enforces a different rule set than the project believes in). The authoritative copy was patched and mirrored,
and the redundant "the two licence definitions match" check was DELETED from the new file: byte-identity is a
stronger statement, and one fact gets one checker.

Three mutations, all red (restore the dangling ref; drift the two definitions; put the bare string back).
Contract layer 60 → 61 files, 1412 → 1421 assertions; run-all.sh 16/16 green.

── ROUND 89: a parameter promised to the model and never passed on ──
The same ruler as the round before, moved to the surface that faces the MODEL: does every parameter a tool
declares actually get read by its handler, and does every parameter a handler reads actually get declared?
Reading the `defineTool` blocks (brace matching; `args.x`, `args?.x` and destructuring all count as a read):

    blender_asset_ingest  unread: ['license']

`blender_asset_ingest` declares `license`, and its description says "Licence string recorded with the asset.
Worth setting for anything downloaded: an asset whose provenance is unrecorded is one nobody can safely ship."
The handler built its request WITHOUT it, and the host had no licence field at all — while the SceneSpec's
asset schema HAS one, so the string had somewhere to go the whole time. This is worse than the config key of
the round before, because the reader who believes the description is a MODEL with no way to find out.

The promise was kept rather than deleted: `ingestAsset` now takes an optional `license`, trims it (capped at
512 characters), writes it into the `assets/manifest.json` entry beside the bytes, returns it, and puts it in
the `asset.add` declaration the caller is told to write — so the licence reaches the SceneSpec, which is where
a later reader looks. When nobody supplied one the field is `null` rather than absent, because "no licence was
given" and "this asset has no licence" are different statements and only the first is true.

THE CHECK ITSELF FELL INTO THREE PITS, each caught by a guard rather than by luck: the declaration has two
spellings (`name: {…}` and `name: SHARED_SHAPE`) and knowing only the first reported `sceneSpec` as read-but-
undeclared; the whole-args allowance matched the `(args, exec)` SIGNATURE and excused all sixteen tools — an
allowance that admits everybody is a hole, so it was deleted and replaced by "every tool must declare at least
one parameter and read at least one"; and taking parameter names by indentation swept up `items:` from the
JSON-Schema spelling of an array parameter, so the parser now takes TOP-LEVEL keys at brace depth 1.

Four mutations, all red (drop the licence again; declare a parameter nothing reads; read an undeclared one;
stop recording the licence in the host). Contract layer 59 → 60 files, 1405 → 1412 assertions.

── ROUND 88: a knob that lied, and a config table that had to be reconciled ──
SPEC §17 illustrates the configuration as NESTED groups (`finalRender: { requireApprovalAboveFrames: 900 }`,
`security: { assetMaxBytes: … }`, `jobs.*`, `agent.*`) while the implementation reads FLAT keys. MEASURED:
Schemastery ACCEPTS the nested document, keeps the group as an unknown property, and reports nothing — so an
operator who follows the spec gets a deployment whose approval threshold is whatever the default was, with no
error anywhere. Silent failure is the one failure mode a configuration must not have.

`contracts/lib/config-surface.js` reads a row's declared keys out of the schema (`dict` sits on the ROOT node,
which is `refs[uid]` — the first version read `json.dict` and declared every key unknown, caught by its own
case) and the three rows (host, provider, ui) now check their own config at construction, refusing an unknown
key and LISTING the keys they do read. `install.md` §3.1 maps all 26 SPEC §17 keys to the real names, the two
unimplemented ones carrying §7 numbers (12: the config shape; 13: `requireApprovalAboveResolution`, whose reason
is that frames are the measure of cost and resolution is only one factor of it).

THE CHECK'S FIRST DAY ON THE JOB CAUGHT TWO THINGS. `serveCachedCapabilities` was declared in the host's schema
and read by NOBODY — the caching it described is the provider's (`capabilitiesCacheMs` plus
`getCapabilities({refresh})`), so a second flag would have been two copies of one decision. It was deleted, and
it turned out to be set in TEN test and tool files, each of which now fails loudly rather than silently doing
nothing: the check turned the cleanup into something verifiable instead of a grep's luck. And `maxMeshPolygons`
was reported as unread because it is read by a COLLABORATOR file in the same package, so the check's scope
became the whole package.

One mutation survived at first and pointed at a real hole: deleting the host's call to the guard changed
nothing, because the case called the helper DIRECTLY and never composed a row — the same shape this session
keeps meeting, where the tool is tested and its use is not. Composing each service with a bad config (and
asserting it throws) turned that mutation, and its provider and ui twins, red.

── ROUND 87: eighteen sentences on the success path, none of them in a manual ──
The same measurement as the round before, one plane over: `recovery.md` has an index by ERROR code, but the
WARNING codes — what a caller reads when the tool returned `ok` — had never been checked. 15 of the 18 appeared
in no manual at all, including `SCENE_COMPILER_DECISION`, which is the one a caller needs most: it says the
pixels came from a spec compiled for this render rather than from the revision's own `.blend`.

`recovery.md` gained §11, one row per warning code, plus how to read them: read the MESSAGE, not only the code,
because the same code says different things in different situations (`SCENE_COMPILER_DECISION` is the example —
a default the compiler chose, or a revision with no checkpoint of its own).

ADDING THAT TABLE BROKE TWO EXISTING CHECKS, and the checks were the thing at fault: they parsed §10's index with
`slice(from the heading)`, which ran to the end of the file. §10 used to be last, so it never mattered; with §11
below it, those checks began reading the warning rows as their own and reported that the index carries
`BLENDER_NOT_INSTALLED`, "which is not a code that exists". The fix was to parse a SECTION (`sectionOf`, stopping
at the next `## `) rather than to touch the table — and the mutation that puts the old slice back turns both
checks red again, so the fix is load-bearing rather than incidental.

Three mutations, all red (delete a warning row; teach a code the build cannot emit; restore the to-EOF slice).
The dark-line reading is unchanged at 38 (0.3%); node:test cases 286 → 287.

── ROUND 86: a twenty-three-word vocabulary the manual had one word of ──
The round began by measuring the wrong thing and saying so: 43 of 62 error codes do not appear in
`recovery.md`, which LOOKS like a hole — but `error-documentation.test.mjs` already keeps a
classification in which every shipped code is either `EXPLAINED` (with the section that tells a reader
what to do) or `NOT_EXPLAINED` under a group that says why a reader never needs it, and it asserts the
classification covers exactly the codes that ship. No hole; nothing changed.

The real hole was one level down: `usage.md` described `blender_scene_patch` — "the only way to change
a scene" — with **one of its twenty-three operation names** anywhere in the file. The schema carries
them for the model, but the manual is what a PERSON reads, and the whole semantics of "change one thing
at a time" rests on those words. A 23-row table was added (operation, required fields, one sentence),
with the field names extracted from `scene-patch.schema.json`'s `oneOf` rather than hand-copied, and
the sentences carrying the parts that bite: `camera.remove` is refused while a shot uses it, `world.set`
REPLACES, `project.frameRange.set` does not touch the scene and so cannot affect the re-render decision,
and removing the last asset makes the key disappear rather than leaving an empty list.

A two-directional check now keeps the two vocabularies equal: a missing row and an invented word both
fail, and the second is worse because it reads as a capability. Writing it, the first regex used
`[a-z.]` and silently dropped `project.frameRange.set` (capital R) — caught by the guard that refuses to
pass when fewer than 23 rows parse. That guard exists for exactly this: a check that passes vacuously is
worse than one that fails.

── ROUND 85: a credential copied into the model's transcript and the job record ──
SPEC §15.2's "日志脱敏" was recorded as half done: no secret reaches a child process, but nothing filtered what
the plugin itself WROTE — and the asset fetch path wrote the URL verbatim in five places: the message the model
reads, the approval prompt the operator reads, and `detail.url` in the job record. A presigned link
(`…?X-Amz-Signature=…`) is the ORDINARY way a model is handed a model file, so "the URL is not a secret" is a
claim this product cannot make.

`contracts/lib/redact.js` drops credentials, the query string and the fragment — and SAYS what it removed. Two
decisions worth stating: it drops rather than MASKS, because masking needs a list of parameter names to trust and
that list is the thing that rots (a signature parameter nobody thought of is a leak, while a dropped query is at
worst a less useful message); and the removal is visible, because a reader comparing the message with what they
pasted must be able to tell a cleaned URL from one that never had a query — a retry against the cleaned one is a
different request. A string that is not a URL cannot be cleaned, so it is bounded (120 characters) instead; that
is not redaction and does not pretend to be.

Seven unit assertions in a new file plus one end-to-end assertion in `host-asset-ingest.test.mjs`: a fetch of a
presigned URL that fails must not carry the signature in its message or in `detail.url`, and must show
"(query removed)". Four mutations, all red (return the URL unchanged; let credentials survive; remove silently;
quote the raw URL again in one message). `redact.js` has no dark lines: the new test walks all of it. Contract
layer 57 → 58 files, 1380 → 1388 assertions.

── ROUND 84: a two-branch sentence whose second branch cannot be reached ──
`renderPreview` built a provenance label with a ternary: "the checkpoint IS this revision" versus "this revision
inherited an earlier checkpoint". The compile path makes the second case impossible — when the nearest checkpoint
is not this revision's, the revision is COMPILED and `resolvedCheckpoint` is the compiled result, whose revision is
the requested one — so the ternary always took the first branch. It sat next to a comment recording a REAL defect
(a spliced word printed "rendered from the revisioncheckpoint" in the ordinary case), and the "rarer inherited
case" that comment mentions no longer exists. The dead arm is deleted and the sentence stays whole; the check that
covered the warning now also asserts the provenance line verbatim and asserts the splice defect's shape is absent.

THE STRANGER PATH WAS RE-MEASURED WITH BLENDER: `npm run verify:clone -- --with-blender` — a fresh clone, a fresh
`DSH_HOME`, the pinned Blender installed by the documented step, the contract suite 57/57 IN the clone, and then
the FULL acceptance suite in the clone: ALL SUITES PASSED. The only warning in that output comes from the harness
rather than from this plugin: `--trace-warnings` points at `@deepseek-ai/dsh-subprocess-local`'s
`process.prependListener('exit', …)`, one per composed Fiber (the hardening suite composes eleven in one process).
Recorded as an upstream observation instead of being silenced with `setMaxListeners(0)`.

The workspace also gained a `.tmp-*` ignore rule: hand-measurement scripts and render directories were showing up
as untracked noise, and a `git status` that cries wolf after every experiment is how a real stray file gets
missed.

── ROUND 91: the fifth wrong answer of the measuring instrument ──
The dark-line total went UP after round 89's fix (39 → 48), and five of the new dark lines were statements that
follow a multi-line TERNARY in the code that fix added. The reading was suspected first, as the rules require: a
temporary `process.stderr.write` on line 3088 printed TWICE, so those lines do run. The raw V8 data showed why
they read dark — line 3087's anchor is covered by three ranges:

  1-4478:1        the module wrapper
  3049-3136:3     the enclosing function, which ran three times
  3083-3092:0     the alternate of the ternary, which never ran AND whose span runs PAST the expression

The merge rule (D112, round 27) judges a line by the innermost range covering its first code character, and the
innermost here is the zero-count alternate — so the five statements after it read as never executed. V8's span is
over-extended; the lines are not part of that expression at all.

BOTH LOOSER RULES WERE MEASURED AND REJECTED rather than assumed: "any positive covering range wins" calls every
named dead branch covered (a dead `errors.push({ … })` body sits inside a function that ran), and "the blackening
zero range starts on an earlier line" is true of 42 of those 48 dark lines, including all the true ones. No span
heuristic separates the two shapes, so the rule stands and the COST IS PAID AT THE SOURCE: the preference is now
an `if`/`else` (a statement produces no such range), after which the five lines read as executed and the only
dark lines left in that method are the `else` arm itself.

That `else` arm is a real state — a record written before `checkpointPath` existed — so a case now drives it:
a hand-written record with no such field resumes, resolves a REAL checkpoint (asserted on the path handed to the
renderer), needs no compile, and completes. Its first version asserted only that the resume WORKED, and the
mutation that deletes the whole `else` arm survived it (`path: null` still renders fine) — the third one-sided
check of this session. Asserting which path reaches the renderer turned the mutation red.

The finding is recorded in `tools/coverage-merge.mjs`'s header, where the instrument's four previous wrong
answers live, so the next reader of a dark list has one instruction: instrument the line before writing a test
for it.

── ROUND 82: a delivery that rendered the WRONG SCENE, found by writing a test ──
An assertion written for something else turned red, and the product was wrong: `startFinalRender` resolved its
checkpoint with `_resolveDeliveryCheckpoint`, which only looks BACKWARDS and never compiles. A revision committed
with `saveCheckpoint: false` therefore rendered the NEAREST EARLIER revision's `.blend`, published those frames
under its own name, and said nothing about it. The preview path had been fixed for exactly this trap — the long
comment above `compileRevisionForRender` is about `saveCheckpoint:false` being a trap rather than a fast path — and
the delivery path had kept a resolver that cannot compile.

The fix: the delivery path resolves like a preview does (`_resolveCheckpointForRender`), so a revision with no
checkpoint of its own is COMPILED and the `SCENE_COMPILER_DECISION` warning names the checkpoint it did not use. The
same preference now applies to `resumeRenderJob` (a resumed attempt must open the same scene). The backward-only
resolver is deleted, and the assertion that used to demand its refusal ("no checkpoint to render from") now demands
the new promise: compile it, and refuse only when the compile produces nothing. Readings from the new case: the
record's `checkpointPath` points at `<workspace>/tmp/render-r0002-…/scene.blend` rather than at r0001's file, and the
delivery manifest records `source.checkpoint.path: null` on purpose — the compile lives in a scratch directory that
is removed afterwards, so the manifest's checkable source is the SceneSpec with its digest.

THE FIX BROKE THE CONCURRENCY SUITE, and that suite was right: awaiting the compile widened the window between "is
another delivery running?" and the job-record write, so two simultaneous deliveries both passed the check and both
wrote a job (under the same allocated id). The rule is now re-checked immediately before the write
(`_assertNoActiveRender`, one copy of the sentence), because the compile is the only slow step in between — and a
mutation that removes that second check puts the concurrency assertions back in the red.

The last mutation also earned its keep by being MIS-AIMED: removing the resume path's new preference reddened
nothing at first, because the case that existed never resumes a compiled render. Writing that case (readings:
`launchesForStart: 1, launchesForResume: 0, resumed: 1`) closed a real gap and turned the mutation red. A mis-aimed
mutation usually points at something nobody is watching.

The dark-line total went UP this round (39 → 48) and that is the honest reading: the fix added a branch whose
neighbouring lines (a second `readFrameLedger` in the same method) the new case does not reach yet, and the deleted
resolver took three product code lines with it.

── ROUND 81: what the Host says when there is nothing to give ──
The Host's last twenty lines are about absence. Restoring a revision committed with `saveCheckpoint: false` must
report `checkpoint: null` rather than a path to a file that was never written — the panel shows that field, and a
path there is a promise that rendering from it will work. A project with `currentRevision: null` (the state
before its first commit finishes) is listed with `scene: null` and is NOT flagged `unreadable`, because "there is
nothing to read" and "reading failed" are two different statements. A job directory with frames but an
unreadable `job.json` is reported as `unreadable` and its record is NOT invented — "corruption is not absence"
(D138) — which also settles a question this round asked: the reconciliation's write closure is never called with
`previous === null`, so that arm is defensive, and the case above is the proof.

One branch was NAMED instead of covered: `exportProject`'s answer carries a ternary whose second arm says
"Delivery encoded but its properties do not match the job's own claims: …", and it cannot be reached —
`_deliverJob` THROWS `ENCODE_VERIFY_FAILED` for `reason: 'export'` before the caller can build that body
(measured: the same job returns through `reason: 'deliver'` and throws through export). It is left in place and
written down, because deleting it would leave a sentence claiming a failed delivery was published.

The round also corrected itself in public: the first version of one assertion read `record.delivery.message`, a
field that does not exist (that sentence belongs to `exportProject`'s answer). The reading printed `undefined`
and the assertion was rewritten around what the record really keeps — `errorCode`, both numbers, and
`_deliverJob`'s own line. `host/lib/index.js` 20 → 18.

── ROUND 80: six best-effort sentences, each with an input that makes it fail ──
`provider-local`'s last fourteen lines were one theme: a SECONDARY thing failing must not fail the primary one.
That kind of sentence is easy to write and easy to delete in a refactor, so each one needs an input that makes
it fail. An "auto" path with no managed install asks for the bare name `blender` rather than handing the string
`auto` to the resolver (driven by telling the provider it has no managed roots — the state of a machine without
the install); a junk allowlist root permits nothing and is SKIPPED, and the observable content of that skip is
that a permitted directory listed AFTER it still matches (the first version asserted only the refusal, and the
mutation turning the `continue` into `return false` survived it — the same one-sided shape as rounds 77 and
78); a captured stream that throws reads as the empty string instead of replacing a classified bootstrap
outcome; a working directory that cannot be removed does not turn a classified answer into a crash; a stale
identity document that cannot be cleared does not refuse a resumed render (a non-empty DIRECTORY where the
file belongs is the one shape `rmSync(..., { force: true })` refuses without `recursive`); and a view whose
bytes cannot be read back is skipped so the views that DID render survive. One branch is named: the
`statSync` guard in `_assertAllowed` sits behind a successful `realpathSync`, so only a file disappearing
between two syscalls reaches it — a TOCTOU race, and the six lines left dark in this file.

Provider-local fell 14 → 6, and those six ARE that race: the file now joins scene-patch and scene-spec in the
"finished" column, where every line is either executed or proved unreachable. Of the nine files still carrying
dark lines, EIGHT (25 lines) are named unreachable branches or named races; only `host/lib/index.js` (20) is
real remaining work.

── ROUND 79: "only the browser suite can reach it" was a statement about the suite ──
`dependency-absent-answers.test.mjs` had said since round 54 that the two `readRequestBody` refusals (an
oversized body, a body that is not a JSON object) and the HTTP-level wrap turning an unknown throw into
`UI_REQUEST_FAILED` all sit "behind the HTTP dispatch, which only the browser suite reaches". That was a
statement about the SUITE, not about the code: `composition/ui-plane.e2e.mjs` has always driven the registered
handler with Node-shaped requests and responses ("no browser, no Blender", says its own header), so the three
branches were reachable the whole time — nobody had driven them. Same repair as round 66: no new seam, just the
cases, plus rewriting the "cannot reach" sentence so the next reader does not inherit it.

What they pin: an unclassified throw (a plain `Error` out of the studio) answers `UI_REQUEST_FAILED` with the
message carried through, status 500 and the real route id — a panel needs something to render, and "this route
blew up in a way nobody named" is actionable where a stack is not; a body over 4 MiB is refused as
`SCENE_PATCH_INVALID` naming the limit, with a 4xx rather than a 500, because a panel that retries a 500
forever never learns that the request itself was too big; JSON that is not an object is refused before any
handler sees it; and `BlenderUiHost.buildCard` IS `buildSettingsCard`, so the panel and the capabilities text
cannot tell two stories about one machine. `ui/lib/index.js` fell 13 → 0 and only nine files carry any dark
lines now.

── ROUND 78: a transaction looking after itself ──
`revision-transaction.js`'s last eight lines were all the same subject: a transaction caring about its OWN
housekeeping — the states nobody arranges by hand. Its staging sweep must clear a crashed transaction's debris
and KEEP the directory the running one owns (sweeping that deletes the directory the commit is writing into,
and a green transaction becomes a missing revision); a staging directory that cannot even be listed sweeps as
nothing instead of throwing mid-commit; an audit record that cannot be written must not replace the error it
was describing; a manifest with no before-hash reports the spec as CHANGED, because "I cannot prove it is
unchanged" must never read as "it is unchanged"; and one operation describes itself in its own words rather
than as "1 operations: …".

One surviving mutation is worth recording again, in the same shape as round 77's: pinning the before-hash
COMPARISON to `false` changed nothing, because that check only asserted the matching case — a `specChanged`
that is always false passed it. The check now commits the same patch twice, the second time against a
before-hash taken from a DIFFERENT document, and asserts both answers; the mutation is red. A one-sided check
of a two-sided question is invisible until something is mutated to the other side.

Finally, two `closeSync` catches (`frame-ledger.js` and `render-journal.js`) are named rather than pretended
covered: the descriptor comes from the `openSync` two lines above, so nothing can close it first, and the note
says what the guard is FOR (a future caller handing in a closed descriptor). `revision-transaction.js` fell
8 → 0; files carrying any dark lines 11 → 10, of which four are branches already proved unreachable and three
are named races. The three real targets left are `host/lib/index.js` (20), `provider-local` (14) and
`ui/lib/index.js` (13).

── ROUND 77: nine small tails, and a sentence that was written twice ──
A round of tails — nine branches scattered across nine files — turned up one real defect, and it was found by a
mutation that SURVIVED. The plan's per-view `purpose` was asserted for the active camera; rewriting
`rolePurpose`'s `case 'active-camera'` to the generic fallback changed nothing, because that sentence was
written a SECOND time at the call site (`claim('active-camera', 'what the animation is actually seen
through', [active])`) while the loop below explicitly SKIPS that role — so the copy inside `rolePurpose` could
never run. One sentence, two homes, and the rotting copy was already dead. The fix is one line (the call site
now asks `rolePurpose`), after which the mutation is red and the sentence has one definition. This is D38/D43's
shape, and it took round 70's rule — a surviving mutation is a question, and mutations must be aimed with
context — to chase it down instead of filing it under "unreachable".

The rest were genuinely tails: `isBlenderError` refuses a look-alike object (the code after it reads
`patchIssue`); `fileSha256` answers the same `null` for absent and unreadable, because "no bytes" is one fact;
an unknown schema `type` throws when the validator RUNS (lazily: buried in a property, only when that property
is reached), which makes the type switch's `default` arm dead code — the check asserts the ORDER and the arm is
named, and the mutation that rewrites it changes nothing, which is the proof; a sheet with no title reserves no
title band, measured in PIXELS because the placement boxes are fractions of the sheet's own height (comparing
the fractions compares two denominators — the first version of that check read a 20 px shift on a 30 px band);
a manifest path outside the delivery root stays absolute rather than being chopped into something that LOOKS
relative; an unreadable journal drains as nothing (and the `statSync` guard above it is a race between two
syscalls, named); proposals that normalise to nothing stop the loop BEFORE it patches; and a camera cap names
the cameras it left out.

Six files went to zero in one round: `visual-loop.js`, `visual-composition.js`, `paths.js`,
`delivery-manifest.js`, `contracts/lib/index.js` and `contact-sheet.js`. The count of files carrying ANY dark
lines fell 17 → 11, and product code lines dark 90 (0.7%) → 74 (0.6%).

── ROUND 76: a pass-through assertion that could not fail, and the guards' own guards ──
Two things in this reading. First, the round re-measured the promise that matters most for a repository
rather than for a file: all four install checks green (`setup:check`, `blender:check`, `plugin:check`,
`presets:check`) and `verify:clone` — the documented four steps on a fresh clone against a fresh
`DSH_HOME`, then the contract suite IN that clone, 57/57.

Second, a surviving mutation taught the shape of a pass-through assertion. The compiler's branch for a
generator shape this build does not know returns the generator untouched; the first version of the check
fed it `{ shape: 'dodecahedron', size: 1 }`, and the mutation that quietly fills in `size: 1` changed
NOTHING on that input — so the mutation lived. Feeding it `size: 7` makes the same assertion red, with the
overwritten `{"shape":"dodecahedron","size":1}` as the reading. To prove "I did nothing", the input must
carry a value the default cannot equal. Three more branches in the same file: the bounding radius of an
unknown shape is 1 × the largest scale (not 0, not a guess — the visual review ranks subjects by it), and
point and spot lights get their own default wattages (100/200) instead of compiling with no energy.

`render-reconciler.js` went 8 → 0 with five answers that are not about a live process: a value that cannot
be a pid is reported `invalid` rather than probed; an empty command line means the identity CANNOT be
established (pids are recycled, which is why the check exists at all); all frames present is still not
complete, because the job owes a video and a manifest — reading it as completed is how a delivery silently
ships without one; an unwritable `recovery.json` must not abort the recovery it describes; and a truncated
`process.json` counts as no identity rather than taking the pass down. The frame fixture paid round 52's
lesson again: a SOLID small PNG compresses to ~100 bytes and is read as truncated, so the frames are
incompressible and written at the resolution the record declares.

── ROUND 75: one claim about what exists, and the store's own guards ──
`validateFindings` already had three of its rejection reasons driven; the last two are the other half of one
sentence — a finding is a claim ABOUT something that exists, and "exists" is checked. An entry that is not
even an object is rejected by name instead of being read for fields (reading `null.category` is how that
survives as a crash), and a finding naming a subject the review does not contain is rejected with the id it
invented. The same round walked through the SECOND door of `assessExposure`: a perfectly reasonable mean with
too many pixels piled at the bottom of the range — what a dark-but-lit product produces — where the sentence
must say THAT rather than claim the mean crossed a floor it did not.

`project-store.js` contributed five guards, each answering instead of throwing: `exists()` answers false for
an id that cannot be a path; `allocateJobId` counts from zero when a project has no jobs directory at all;
an artifact recorded into a revision with NO manifest is returned without inventing a manifest; an
idempotency record holding a different key reads as no record rather than as somebody else's outcome; and a
title whose ids are all taken is refused after 1000 attempts — a check that ALSO counts the attempts, because
a sentence saying "after 1000 attempts" while the loop gave up after three is a sentence nobody can trust.
One branch is NAMED rather than pretended covered: the `catch {}` around the derived index write has no
driver in this layer (the cheap way to fail the write fails the READ first — measured — a read-only root
would take the project directory with it, and the index path cannot be redirected).

Both files went to zero: `visual-issue.js` 8 → 0 and `project-store.js` 12 → 0. `tool/lib/render-tools.js` fell 11 → 0 in two steps, and the step in between is the lesson: the first reading left ONE line, the no-frame-range arm of the approval sentence, because that case had supplied a range and only half the sentence ran. A second approval case — a frame COUNT with no range, asserting the prompt never contains `undefined` — closed it. A reading's tail is usually the next test case.

Round 30 recorded a delivery that failed to encode while its record still said "encoding"; round 31
drove the reconciler's three safety rules with real processes; round 32 made a cited probe reproducible;
